WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.966
Our data have a within-sector log-sales
variance of nineteen point nine zero four.

2
00:00:05.966 --> 00:00:08.126
This horizontal target stays fixed.

3
00:00:08.126 --> 00:00:11.582
We vary sigma, the dispersion of log
productivity.

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.082
In this simple example, the model variance

5
00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:19.375
has an exact formula: eta minus one,
squared, times sigma squared.

6
00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:23.277
Moving sigma moves the predicted moment
along the blue curve.

7
00:00:23.277 --> 00:00:26.789
The vertical gap measures its distance
from the data.

8
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.657
SMM computes that same statistic in
simulated data.

9
00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:35.790
Here we reuse one saved simulated data set
across candidates.

10
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.097
Its orange curve differs from the exact
moment.

11
00:00:39.097 --> 00:00:43.230
At sigma one, both predictions exceed the
same observed target.

12
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.257
Now square those vertical gaps.

13
00:00:46.257 --> 00:00:48.926
The two loss curves reach zero at

14
00:00:48.926 --> 00:00:53.883
different parameter values: about zero
point nine three nine for direct GMM, and

15
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.934
zero point nine one seven for this
simulation.

16
00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.222
These are the saved continuous estimates.

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.415
Averaging one thousand saved replications

18
00:01:02.415 --> 00:01:06.958
brings the simulated curve and estimate
close to the direct result.

19
00:01:06.958 --> 00:01:08.611
The criterion is unchanged.

20
00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:11.502
Simulation changes how we obtain the
moment.
